WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:04.404
- They value safety and Dave,

00:00:04.404 --> 00:00:07.707
the illusion of safety

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:11.444 align:start size:84% position:16%
more than they value
personal liberty.

00:00:11.444 --> 00:00:14.381
And that's why they
went along with it.

00:00:14.381 --> 00:00:19.386 align:start size:84% position:16%
I have very, very many
intelligent friends

00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:21.755
who just couldn't wait
to put that mask on.

00:00:24.024 --> 00:00:26.693
(upbeat music)

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:37.971 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin
and joining me today

00:00:37.971 --> 00:00:40.874 line:0%
is a former New Jersey
superior court judge

00:00:40.874 --> 00:00:44.177 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
and host of the "Judging
Freedom" podcast,

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:45.612
Judge Andrew Napolitano.

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:46.913
Welcome to the Rubin report.

00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:48.748
- A pleasure to be
on with you, Dave,

00:00:48.748 --> 00:00:50.183
thank you for having me.

00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:51.451
- I'm glad to have you.

00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:54.120 align:start size:84% position:16%
Before we dive into
the issues of the day,

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.523 align:start position:0%
and there's a lot of stuff sort
of culturally, politically,

00:00:56.523 --> 00:00:59.626
but especially legally that
I want to talk to you about.

00:00:59.626 --> 00:01:01.728 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's a rumor
that you might be up

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:03.496
on a farm somewhere in
the middle of nowhere.

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:05.598
And I find that
to be interesting

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:07.834
considering most people
picture you with a suit,

00:01:07.834 --> 00:01:10.003
slicked back hair, some makeup,

00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:11.871 align:start size:84% position:16%
but you like living
on a farm, huh?

00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:15.875 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- Well, for 24 years, I
was very happy at Fox News.

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:17.310 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
They put me in
front of a camera.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.411 line:0%
You ready for this?

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:21.714
14,500 times.

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:24.751 align:start size:84% position:16%
And they have the
clothes police there,

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:26.719
really wonderful people,

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:28.621 align:start size:84% position:16%
but made sure you
were dressed properly.

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:31.491
And the makeup was right
and your hair was right.

00:01:31.491 --> 00:01:34.794 align:start size:84% position:16%
Since COVID and then
after I left Fox,

00:01:34.794 --> 00:01:36.229 align:start size:88% position:13%
I've been doing
everything from my farm,

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:37.831
which is where I am now,

00:01:37.831 --> 00:01:40.533 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is at the northwest
tip of New Jersey,

00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:43.303 align:start size:97% position:3%
where New York, New Jersey,
and Pennsylvania meet.

00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:46.673
We average about 15 degrees
colder than New York City.

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:49.442 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I've left here in a
blizzard and got to Manhattan

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:50.710
and it's just rain.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.313
We're we're about 1,800
feet above sea level.

00:01:55.115 --> 00:01:56.916
- And what are you
doing up on a farm?

00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:57.884
I mean, are you out there?

00:01:57.884 --> 00:02:00.286
Are you pulling up vegetables

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:01.888
and you got some
animals out there,

00:02:01.888 --> 00:02:03.223
what are you doing?

00:02:03.223 --> 00:02:04.724
- I sign the checks,

00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:08.328
the wonderful guys that work
for me get dirty fingernails.

00:02:08.328 --> 00:02:10.497
We just finished
making maple syrup.

00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:14.000
We grow, we have an apple
orchard and a pear orchard,

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:19.005 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we grow about
500 pounds of garlic.

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:22.509 align:start size:84% position:16%
And we grow a lot
of green vegetables.

00:02:22.509 --> 00:02:26.913
Our clients are hotels and
restaurants in the area.

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:28.281
It's a lot of fun.

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:31.451 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's given me an appreciation
for the environment

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:35.088
that I never had before
I had this property.

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:36.322
- Yeah, very cool.

00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:37.724
I just thought it was
worth mentioning up top

00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:39.325
because I think when
people see people,

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:40.793
especially on cable news,

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:43.496 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's something about
seeing the person in the box

00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:46.299
where you don't realize that
they're actually a person too,

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:49.402 align:start size:88% position:13%
that they just appear
to be this head floating

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:50.670
with a fancy background.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.805
- Well like you, I am
very much a person.

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:54.340
- You are very much a person.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.177
All right, well, let's do
a little bit of the person.

00:02:57.177 --> 00:03:00.513 align:start size:84% position:16%
So former superior
court judge in Jersey,

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:04.651
what got you interested in
the law in the first place?

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:06.286
How does someone
end up as a judge?

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:08.221
People think of law,
you become a lawyer.

00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:10.723
How did you end up as a
judge in the first place?

00:03:10.723 --> 00:03:11.958
- Well I ended up as a lawyer

00:03:11.958 --> 00:03:13.893 align:start size:84% position:16%
because at dinner
table conversations,

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:15.595 align:start size:84% position:16%
I always took the
side of the underdog,

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:18.097
much to my parents' dismay.

00:03:19.499 --> 00:03:23.469
I loved debating in high
school and even in college.

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:27.173
So I went to Princeton and one
of my classmates, Sam Alito,

00:03:27.173 --> 00:03:28.408
one of my boyhood friends is now

00:03:28.408 --> 00:03:31.377
on the Supreme Court
of the United States.

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:35.515
New Jersey's system is a little
different than other states.

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:37.317
You practice law for 10 years.

00:03:37.317 --> 00:03:40.720
And then after that are
eligible to be appointed.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.289
I was the youngest
person appointed

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:44.524
in the history of the state.

00:03:44.524 --> 00:03:47.160
I was actually appointed
before I had completed

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.428
that 10 year mark,

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:51.264
but I didn't take office
until after I completed.

00:03:51.264 --> 00:03:52.966
Appointed by the governor,

00:03:52.966 --> 00:03:56.102
confirmed by the state
senate for seven years,

00:03:56.102 --> 00:03:59.205 align:start size:97% position:3%
if reappointed and reconfirmed
before the end of seven,

00:03:59.205 --> 00:04:00.740
you have it for life.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:04.377 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I was appointed by a
wonderful moderate Republican,

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:06.212
Governor Tom Kane,

00:04:06.212 --> 00:04:09.582 align:start position:0%
and then seven years later
received my lifetime appointment

00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:12.986
by another wonderful
moderate Republican,

00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:14.821
Governor Christine Todd Whitman.

00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:17.890
At the trial level where I was,

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.426
where there are 400
judges in the state,

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:24.230 align:start size:97% position:3%
there are roughly 200
Republicans and 200 Democrats.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:25.798
So it's not political

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:28.301
because it's not a
policy making court.

00:04:28.301 --> 00:04:30.903
You're trying cases, you're
deciding who you believe.

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:32.639
You're not making decisions

00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:36.876
like the Supreme Court of
the United States does.

00:04:36.876 --> 00:04:39.912 align:start size:88% position:13%
- How did you make
sure that your politics

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:41.581
didn't leak into it?

00:04:41.581 --> 00:04:43.016 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean I have a sense
from watching you

00:04:43.016 --> 00:04:44.717 align:start size:84% position:16%
over the years of what
your politics are,

00:04:44.717 --> 00:04:46.919
but I know you try to
be, as on your show,

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:48.888 align:start size:88% position:13%
you try to be as
nonpartisan as possible,

00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:50.957
but obviously people
have their own biases,

00:04:50.957 --> 00:04:52.625
have their own ways of
looking at the world.

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:54.093
How did you do it?

00:04:54.093 --> 00:04:57.063
And do you think that enough
judges are doing it these days?

00:04:57.063 --> 00:04:58.498
Because it does seem these days,

00:04:58.498 --> 00:05:01.167 align:start size:97% position:3%
that courts seem to be slanted
one one way or another.

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:05.605
- Well, I, like you, believe
that our rights are natural

00:05:05.605 --> 00:05:07.540
and come from our humanity,

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.742 align:start size:84% position:16%
whether you believe
in God as I do

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:11.244
and that it's a gift from God,

00:05:11.244 --> 00:05:13.079
or whether you believe we
have developed these rights

00:05:13.079 --> 00:05:15.014
by our own exercise of reason,

00:05:15.014 --> 00:05:16.916 align:start size:84% position:16%
they don't come
from the government.

00:05:16.916 --> 00:05:19.886
They're not privileges,
they're not licenses,

00:05:19.886 --> 00:05:22.255
they're rights, they're claims.

00:05:22.255 --> 00:05:25.825
And therefore I believe
that the constitution

00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:28.227
literally means what it says.

00:05:28.227 --> 00:05:31.097 align:start position:0%
If the government wants to take
life, liberty, or property,

00:05:31.097 --> 00:05:33.900
it has to follow due process,

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:35.034
which means a jury trial

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:36.502 align:start size:88% position:13%
at which the government
proves fault,

00:05:36.502 --> 00:05:39.238
it doesn't mean an edict
thou shalt wear a mask.

00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:41.240
Thou shalt take a vaccine.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:43.743
Thou shalt not stand
closer than six feet

00:05:43.743 --> 00:05:45.211
from the next person.

00:05:45.211 --> 00:05:47.880 align:start size:88% position:13%
Thou shalt not open your
restaurant business.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.551
All of those in my view are
unconstitutional, unlawful,

00:05:51.551 --> 00:05:56.022 align:start size:97% position:3%
and immoral because they
violate your natural rights.

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:58.925
Does that seep into
the judicial process?

00:05:58.925 --> 00:06:00.426
Yes, it does.

00:06:00.426 --> 00:06:03.463
Before a criminal trial,

00:06:03.463 --> 00:06:05.665 align:start size:88% position:13%
the government must
demonstrate to the court

00:06:05.665 --> 00:06:08.801
how it acquired the
evidence in the case,

00:06:08.801 --> 00:06:10.670 align:start size:94% position:6%
because the Supreme Court
of the United States

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:13.172
and the Supreme Court of
New Jersey have both rule.

00:06:13.172 --> 00:06:15.308
If the evidence was
obtained unlawfully,

00:06:15.308 --> 00:06:17.076 align:start size:88% position:13%
then it's that the
police broke of the law,

00:06:17.076 --> 00:06:19.645 align:start size:88% position:13%
when they're ostensibly
enforcing the law,

00:06:19.645 --> 00:06:22.782
then the evidence can't
be introduced at trial.

00:06:22.782 --> 00:06:27.220 align:start size:88% position:13%
If you are a natural
law libertarian as I am,

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:31.491
you tend to follow that to a T.

00:06:31.491 --> 00:06:32.992
Translated,

00:06:32.992 --> 00:06:35.428
I probably suppressed more
evidence than all the other

00:06:35.428 --> 00:06:37.597 align:start size:84% position:16%
judges in the
courthouse combined.

00:06:37.597 --> 00:06:39.932 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I was not the
prosecutor's favorite

00:06:39.932 --> 00:06:41.534
except on sentencings,

00:06:41.534 --> 00:06:44.137 align:start position:0%
then they loved me because
there's a wing of the New Jersey

00:06:44.137 --> 00:06:48.207
state prison with my name on
it because I sent so many bad,

00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:50.076
violent people there,

00:06:50.076 --> 00:06:53.146 align:start size:88% position:13%
not people who committed
harmless crimes,

00:06:53.146 --> 00:06:55.214 align:start size:84% position:16%
but people who
committed real crimes.

00:06:55.214 --> 00:07:00.219 align:start size:94% position:6%
So yes, it's more a
philosophy than a politics

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:06.058 align:start size:84% position:16%
that filters into
the judging process.

00:07:06.926 --> 00:07:08.127
- [Dave] Do you remember-

00:07:08.127 --> 00:07:09.395 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Having said that, much
of it is mechanical.

00:07:09.395 --> 00:07:10.930
You're trying a case.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:13.800
You wanna make sure
the jury understands.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.969
You wanna make sure both
sides get a fair shake.

00:07:16.969 --> 00:07:18.304
Even in sentencing,

00:07:18.304 --> 00:07:19.872
talking about Jussie Smollett

00:07:19.872 --> 00:07:22.074
three or four times this week.

00:07:22.074 --> 00:07:23.810 align:start size:84% position:16%
Had an unusual
twist the other day,

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:25.611
when the appellate court
let him out for the reason

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:27.079
for which they let him out.

00:07:27.079 --> 00:07:29.482
But when judges sentence, Dave,

00:07:29.482 --> 00:07:32.819
they have a checklist,
25 aggravating factors,

00:07:32.819 --> 00:07:34.687
25 mitigating factors.

00:07:34.687 --> 00:07:36.055 align:start size:84% position:16%
So you're not shooting
from your hip.

00:07:36.055 --> 00:07:38.724 align:start size:84% position:16%
You're deciding do the
aggravating factors

00:07:38.724 --> 00:07:40.193
outweigh the mitigating factors.

00:07:40.193 --> 00:07:43.129
Is this a human being
who really is a danger

00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:45.932 align:start size:84% position:16%
to society and needs
to be locked up?

00:07:45.932 --> 00:07:48.901
Or is this somebody who
just got carried away,

00:07:48.901 --> 00:07:51.604
got involved in something
and it got outta control

00:07:51.604 --> 00:07:54.474 align:start size:97% position:3%
and he didn't have the courage
to admit that it was wrong.

00:07:54.474 --> 00:07:57.210
You have to make those objective

00:07:57.210 --> 00:07:58.811
and subjective judgements

00:07:58.811 --> 00:08:01.147 align:start size:88% position:13%
before you take
someone's freedom away.

00:08:01.147 --> 00:08:04.150 align:start size:94% position:6%
- So I wanna get more
Smollett in just a minute,

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:05.618 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I find that really
interesting about,

00:08:05.618 --> 00:08:08.154 align:start size:94% position:6%
so you really tried to
keep the law of the letter

00:08:08.154 --> 00:08:10.756
regarding policing and if
they broke the law, okay,

00:08:10.756 --> 00:08:12.391
the evidence can't be submitted.

00:08:12.391 --> 00:08:13.926 align:start size:84% position:16%
Were there times,
I'm sure there were,

00:08:13.926 --> 00:08:16.329
that you saw something
that was so damning,

00:08:16.329 --> 00:08:18.431
so incriminating, and
you're going, man,

00:08:18.431 --> 00:08:20.833 align:start size:81% position:19%
if I don't let
this be submitted,

00:08:20.833 --> 00:08:23.336 align:start size:88% position:13%
there's a really bad
dude who's about to walk

00:08:23.336 --> 00:08:25.304 align:start size:84% position:16%
and yet you'd still
have to do it.

00:08:25.304 --> 00:08:27.673
- Yes, yes you do.

00:08:27.673 --> 00:08:30.109 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I talked to
police about this.

00:08:30.109 --> 00:08:34.180
Police who have beaten
confessions out of people

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:37.683
or cracked a taillight
after they stop the car

00:08:37.683 --> 00:08:40.887 align:start size:97% position:3%
because they got a tip that
drugs were in the trunk

00:08:40.887 --> 00:08:44.991 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they needed a lawful basis
for smashing the taillight.

00:08:44.991 --> 00:08:46.893 align:start size:84% position:16%
I said to one of
them, I said, you know

00:08:46.893 --> 00:08:49.295
you and I grew up in
the same neighborhood.

00:08:49.295 --> 00:08:50.997 align:start size:84% position:16%
I didn't come down
with yesterday's rain.

00:08:50.997 --> 00:08:53.132 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know I was gonna
see through this.

00:08:53.132 --> 00:08:55.935 align:start size:88% position:13%
And he said, yes, here's
how we look at it.

00:08:55.935 --> 00:08:58.871
We got this stuff
off the street.

00:08:58.871 --> 00:09:01.774
We know a guilty guy went free

00:09:01.774 --> 00:09:03.109
and he's probably
gonna do it again.

00:09:03.109 --> 00:09:04.310
And we'll get him the next time.

00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:05.978 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the next time
we'll follow the law,

00:09:05.978 --> 00:09:10.116
but we just took 10 pounds
of cocaine off the street.

00:09:10.116 --> 00:09:12.718
Our problem was that
we got assigned to you

00:09:12.718 --> 00:09:14.554
because you're the only
judge in the courthouse

00:09:14.554 --> 00:09:17.089 align:start size:84% position:16%
that would have caught
us for doing this.

00:09:17.089 --> 00:09:19.625
So there are factors to weigh,

00:09:19.625 --> 00:09:24.297 align:start size:84% position:16%
but my oath is to
the constitution when
I was on the bench.

00:09:24.297 --> 00:09:27.567
It's not to the government,
it's not to the prosecutors,

00:09:27.567 --> 00:09:29.835
it's not to the police,
it's to the constitution.

00:09:29.835 --> 00:09:32.338 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's the constitution
that mandates

00:09:32.338 --> 00:09:35.141
that illegally obtained
evidence can't be used

00:09:35.141 --> 00:09:37.310
in a trial in America.

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:39.545
If it could, there
would be no trials.

00:09:39.545 --> 00:09:41.781 align:start size:94% position:6%
Everybody would plead
guilty because the police

00:09:41.781 --> 00:09:44.083
would beat the crap
out of everybody.

00:09:44.083 --> 00:09:46.252
- So I wanna get to
the issues of the day

00:09:46.252 --> 00:09:48.788 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we'll talk about Smollett
and the Supreme Court

00:09:48.788 --> 00:09:50.256 align:start size:84% position:16%
justice nominee
and all that stuff.

00:09:50.256 --> 00:09:54.160 align:start size:94% position:6%
But so what possessed
you to go from the court,

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:55.661
which you obviously loved.

00:09:55.661 --> 00:09:57.229 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's very obvious
hearing you talk about it

00:09:57.229 --> 00:09:59.832
to saying hey I'll do
this on television,

00:09:59.832 --> 00:10:01.500
which is a whole other game.

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:03.469 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Well I'm a little
embarrassed to tell you

00:10:03.469 --> 00:10:06.405
except it's public knowledge
because I said it at the time,

00:10:07.707 --> 00:10:09.241
I was tired of being poor.

00:10:11.944 --> 00:10:15.781
So judges at the time made a
hundred thousand dollars a year

00:10:15.781 --> 00:10:18.351
with zero outside income.

00:10:18.351 --> 00:10:19.752
In addition to being a judge,

00:10:19.752 --> 00:10:22.088
I was a full-time law professor,

00:10:22.088 --> 00:10:23.322
but teaching just at night

00:10:23.322 --> 00:10:26.892
so it didn't interfere
with my judicial duties.

00:10:26.892 --> 00:10:29.428
I was also lecturing
around the country

00:10:29.428 --> 00:10:31.364
about the types of things
that we're talking about,

00:10:31.364 --> 00:10:32.932
human liberty and
natural rights.

00:10:32.932 --> 00:10:36.135 align:start size:97% position:3%
And one day one of my
clerks, a very bright person

00:10:36.135 --> 00:10:39.005 align:start size:84% position:16%
said to me do you know
if you could charge

00:10:39.005 --> 00:10:41.907
the fair market value for
this stuff you do at night,

00:10:41.907 --> 00:10:43.509
it would be two and a half times

00:10:43.509 --> 00:10:46.812
what the state is paying you
for what you do during the day.

00:10:46.812 --> 00:10:48.381
Eh, that was it.

00:10:48.381 --> 00:10:52.018 align:start size:88% position:13%
I didn't leave the bench
to go on television.

00:10:52.018 --> 00:10:56.555 line:0%
I left the bench to go back
into the pits and try cases.

00:10:59.692 --> 00:11:00.893
Eight or nine years before,

00:11:00.893 --> 00:11:05.631
I had met Roger Ailes
at the Kansas City

00:11:05.631 --> 00:11:09.001
Republican National
Convention in 1976,

00:11:09.001 --> 00:11:11.404 align:start size:84% position:16%
Ronald Reagan
versus Gerald Ford.

00:11:12.805 --> 00:11:15.808 align:start size:97% position:3%
Roger was the chief strategist
for one of the two camps.

00:11:15.808 --> 00:11:17.977
I'll let you guess
which camp it was.

00:11:21.781 --> 00:11:24.150 align:start size:97% position:3%
Almost everybody says Reagan,
he was on Ford's side.

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:25.951
- No well that's why I paused

00:11:25.951 --> 00:11:28.387 align:start position:0%
because I thought you were
setting me up for a trap there.

00:11:28.387 --> 00:11:29.822
- Right.

00:11:29.822 --> 00:11:31.057 align:start size:97% position:3%
Well Ford was the President
of the United States.

00:11:31.057 --> 00:11:33.092
Anyway, that's where
I met Roger Ailes.

00:11:33.092 --> 00:11:34.660
I left the bench.

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:38.064 align:start size:94% position:6%
The statement about
being poor had such legs,

00:11:38.064 --> 00:11:39.432
it was all over the place.

00:11:39.432 --> 00:11:41.801
Life tenured judge
leaves because of,

00:11:43.202 --> 00:11:45.237 align:start size:97% position:3%
a poor salary and you can't
earn an outside income

00:11:45.237 --> 00:11:46.806
and people dumped on me.

00:11:46.806 --> 00:11:48.207
What do you mean a hundred
thousand dollars is poor.

00:11:48.207 --> 00:11:50.276
That's more than 90%
of the country makes

00:11:50.276 --> 00:11:51.677
blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:11:51.677 --> 00:11:54.080
Roger Ailes read about it.

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:56.148 align:start size:84% position:16%
OJ Simpson had
just been indicted.

00:11:57.383 --> 00:12:00.319
Ailes was then the
president of CNBC.

00:12:00.319 --> 00:12:02.455
He said we're gonna cover this.

00:12:02.455 --> 00:12:05.491
And I'm gonna put my
own ex-judge on air

00:12:05.491 --> 00:12:09.128
to second guess this
crazy loopy Judge Ito.

00:12:09.128 --> 00:12:11.263
So for five days a week

00:12:12.665 --> 00:12:17.570 align:start position:0%
with my future colleague at
Fox, John Gibson, we covered OJ.

00:12:18.738 --> 00:12:20.372
John in the courtroom
and interviewing people

00:12:20.372 --> 00:12:23.476
outside the courthouse
and me providing analysis

00:12:23.476 --> 00:12:28.280
for CNBC at their offices in
Englewood Cliffs, New Jersey.

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:32.251
Then Microsoft and
NBC formed MSNBC.

00:12:33.419 --> 00:12:36.021 align:start size:84% position:16%
Roger did not get
the presidency of it,

00:12:36.021 --> 00:12:39.125 align:start size:84% position:16%
probably the worst
mistake NBC ever made.

00:12:39.125 --> 00:12:42.695
He left, formed Fox,
and brought those of us

00:12:42.695 --> 00:12:44.830 align:start size:84% position:16%
who had worked for him
at CNBC over to Fox

00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:46.298
basically said I don't
know where it's gonna go.

00:12:46.298 --> 00:12:47.533
It's a gamble.

00:12:47.533 --> 00:12:49.335 align:start size:84% position:16%
We could all be
bankrupt in a week.

00:12:49.335 --> 00:12:52.605
Now that was 25, 26 years ago.

00:12:52.605 --> 00:12:56.275
- And did you feel
covering from OJ

00:12:56.275 --> 00:12:58.677
to all the stuff that you
cover, were covering on Fox

00:12:58.677 --> 00:13:01.580
for 15, 20 years, actually more.

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:03.716
Did you feel that
there is a risk

00:13:03.716 --> 00:13:05.584
in doing some of that day to day

00:13:05.584 --> 00:13:09.789 align:start size:97% position:3%
on cable news that you
have to be more sensational

00:13:09.789 --> 00:13:13.125 align:start size:97% position:3%
or that you maybe aren't
privy to all the information

00:13:13.125 --> 00:13:14.527
that's going on
in the courtroom.

00:13:14.527 --> 00:13:16.662 align:start size:88% position:13%
Or you gotta keep people
watching and know,

00:13:16.662 --> 00:13:18.330 align:start size:88% position:13%
you know one of the
things that I find tough

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:20.065 align:start size:84% position:16%
when you follow court
cases these days,

00:13:20.065 --> 00:13:22.201
the way everyone seems
to do on cable news,

00:13:22.201 --> 00:13:24.570
we're suddenly privy to
all of this information

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:26.739
that maybe we all shouldn't
be privy to all the time,

00:13:26.739 --> 00:13:28.340
or at least the public
shouldn't care about

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:30.609
every little in and out
of every little thing.

00:13:30.609 --> 00:13:32.211
How do you try to blend that?

00:13:32.211 --> 00:13:33.612 align:start size:81% position:19%
- I agree with you
that the public

00:13:33.612 --> 00:13:36.015
shouldn't care about
every little in and out

00:13:36.015 --> 00:13:37.283
and every little thing,

00:13:37.283 --> 00:13:39.919
but in this era of
cable news, we do.

00:13:39.919 --> 00:13:42.488 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't know how the founding
fathers would've fared

00:13:42.488 --> 00:13:44.990 align:start size:84% position:16%
if cable news had been
around at the time.

00:13:44.990 --> 00:13:47.726
- Not well, not well.

00:13:47.726 --> 00:13:51.664 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Fox had and has
terrific researchers.

00:13:51.664 --> 00:13:53.098
They also allowed me

00:13:53.098 --> 00:13:57.670 align:start size:84% position:16%
to hire my own
researcher, a lawyer.

00:13:57.670 --> 00:13:59.505
In between Fox's researchers,

00:13:59.505 --> 00:14:01.841 align:start size:84% position:16%
known affectionately
as the brain room

00:14:01.841 --> 00:14:03.275
and my researcher,

00:14:03.275 --> 00:14:08.214
I was able to devour
what I needed to know.

00:14:08.214 --> 00:14:09.481
Occasionally you make a mistake,

00:14:09.481 --> 00:14:12.785
sometimes a mistake
leads to a lawsuit.

00:14:12.785 --> 00:14:16.789 align:start position:0%
But remember you are explaining
things for the public,

00:14:16.789 --> 00:14:19.258 align:start size:97% position:3%
you're not making an argument
before the Supreme Court.

00:14:19.258 --> 00:14:22.061
So you don't need
to know everything.

00:14:22.061 --> 00:14:25.331 align:start position:0%
The key is to explain it so
that the public understands it,

00:14:25.331 --> 00:14:27.900 align:start size:97% position:3%
appreciates the explanation,
and wants more of it.

00:14:28.901 --> 00:14:30.469
- Okay so with that in mind,

00:14:30.469 --> 00:14:32.771
let's switch to some of
the issues of the day.

00:14:32.771 --> 00:14:34.340
So you brought up the
Jussie Smollett thing,

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:37.142 align:start size:97% position:3%
which we covered a whole
bunch over the last few weeks.

00:14:37.142 --> 00:14:38.844
So he gets sentenced,

00:14:38.844 --> 00:14:41.513 align:start size:88% position:13%
there's really no one
other than him I suppose

00:14:41.513 --> 00:14:43.782 align:start size:97% position:3%
and his lawyer who's claiming
that he's still innocent.

00:14:43.782 --> 00:14:45.684
I mean it's sort of bananas.

00:14:45.684 --> 00:14:47.720
And everyone saw the
diatribe at the end

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:49.455
during the sentencing.

00:14:49.455 --> 00:14:52.258
He gets the 150 days in jail
and a couple other things,

00:14:52.258 --> 00:14:54.426 align:start size:88% position:13%
but basically he's gonna
serve a week in jail

00:14:54.426 --> 00:14:56.395 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that's it, they're
gonna let him out.

00:14:56.395 --> 00:15:00.165
What do you make
of the sentencing,

00:15:00.165 --> 00:15:02.034
then the shortening
of the sentencing,

00:15:02.034 --> 00:15:04.803
and just the media part of this.

00:15:04.803 --> 00:15:06.405
And how do you think
the judge had to deal

00:15:06.405 --> 00:15:08.073
with the media portion of it?

00:15:08.073 --> 00:15:10.910 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Okay the media is
not easy to deal with.

00:15:10.910 --> 00:15:13.612
In the era that I was
on the bench, Dave,

00:15:13.612 --> 00:15:16.949 align:start size:88% position:13%
cameras were not
permitted in courtrooms

00:15:16.949 --> 00:15:19.919
and in the few courtrooms in
which they were permitted,

00:15:19.919 --> 00:15:22.554
the judge had an absolute
right to exclude them.

00:15:22.554 --> 00:15:24.356
Today you can't exclude them.

00:15:24.356 --> 00:15:26.125 align:start size:84% position:16%
In federal court
they're not permitted,

00:15:26.125 --> 00:15:28.994 align:start size:97% position:3%
but in state courts,
they're there all the time.

00:15:28.994 --> 00:15:30.262
They're tiny.

00:15:30.262 --> 00:15:32.131
They don't make any
noise after a while.

00:15:32.131 --> 00:15:34.133 align:start size:94% position:6%
You forget that they're
there, but they are there,

00:15:34.133 --> 00:15:37.836
which is what we all know more
detail than we need to know.

00:15:37.836 --> 00:15:40.205 align:start size:84% position:16%
I thought his
sentencing was fair.

00:15:40.205 --> 00:15:45.210
Normally, lying to the police
does not implicate jail time.

00:15:46.612 --> 00:15:49.148 align:start size:94% position:6%
I have a problem with
punishing people for lying

00:15:49.148 --> 00:15:51.116
to the police or
lying to the FBI

00:15:51.116 --> 00:15:56.121
because the law allows the FBI
and the police to lie to you.

00:15:56.855 --> 00:15:58.123
So if they can lie-

00:15:58.123 --> 00:15:59.358
Do you remember the
Martha Stewart case?

00:15:59.358 --> 00:16:01.093
They lied to her in
the same conversation

00:16:01.093 --> 00:16:02.328
in which she lied to them.

00:16:02.328 --> 00:16:05.164
The FBI agent who lied
to her got a promotion.

00:16:05.164 --> 00:16:08.467
She went to jail for six
months for lying to them.

00:16:08.467 --> 00:16:09.868
So I have a problem with that.

00:16:09.868 --> 00:16:11.270
- Quick sidebar on that,

00:16:11.270 --> 00:16:14.273
I was a PA on Martha
Stewart's talk show

00:16:14.273 --> 00:16:16.542
when she came back
after jail time,

00:16:16.542 --> 00:16:19.278
and she still had the
monitor on her foot

00:16:19.278 --> 00:16:21.180
when she was doing the show.

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:22.514
- When she came to Fox,

00:16:22.514 --> 00:16:25.317
she asked for me and
hugged and kissed me.

00:16:25.317 --> 00:16:27.820
We are from two towns
next to each other,

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:30.823
Bloomfield, New Jersey
and Nutley, New Jersey.

00:16:30.823 --> 00:16:33.058 align:start size:84% position:16%
Bloomfield very
Italian at the time,

00:16:33.058 --> 00:16:33.993
Nutley very Polish.

00:16:33.993 --> 00:16:35.427
- I know Bloomfield well.

00:16:35.427 --> 00:16:36.895 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Her name is no more
Stewart than mine is Smith,

00:16:36.895 --> 00:16:41.266 align:start size:84% position:16%
but she promoted
that very, very well.

00:16:41.266 --> 00:16:42.101
- Got it.

00:16:42.101 --> 00:16:43.168
- Now back to Jussie.

00:16:44.536 --> 00:16:47.506 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because the lies were regular,
consistent, systematic,

00:16:47.506 --> 00:16:51.577 align:start size:94% position:6%
and because the government
wasted 125 to $150,000

00:16:52.578 --> 00:16:54.279
investigating the lies,

00:16:54.279 --> 00:16:57.716
I believe that the jail
sentence of 150 days

00:16:57.716 --> 00:17:00.052
and the restitution to the
government was appropriate.

00:17:00.052 --> 00:17:02.154
Now his sentence
was not shortened.

00:17:02.154 --> 00:17:04.957
He was let off on appeal.

00:17:04.957 --> 00:17:08.127
It's an interesting reason
why it was let off on appeal

00:17:08.127 --> 00:17:11.630 align:start size:97% position:3%
because if he started
serving 150 days immediately,

00:17:11.630 --> 00:17:13.699 align:start size:94% position:6%
he would be finished
with it before the appeal

00:17:13.699 --> 00:17:15.667
would ever even be heard.

00:17:15.667 --> 00:17:18.670
Because it takes a year or
two for the appellate courts

00:17:18.670 --> 00:17:21.974 align:start size:97% position:3%
to hear your appeal because
the appeals get online

00:17:21.974 --> 00:17:24.676 align:start size:84% position:16%
on the basis of when
they were filed.

00:17:24.676 --> 00:17:28.714 align:start size:84% position:16%
So he's still facing
150 days in jail

00:17:28.714 --> 00:17:30.816 align:start size:94% position:6%
unless the appellate
court decides either that

00:17:30.816 --> 00:17:34.620
he was wrongfully convicted
or that he was over punished.

00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:36.722 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't think
either is the case.

00:17:36.722 --> 00:17:37.656
- Right.

00:17:37.656 --> 00:17:38.991
In a situation like that,

00:17:38.991 --> 00:17:41.026
so when they finally get to it,

00:17:41.026 --> 00:17:42.895
do they usually just say, ah,

00:17:42.895 --> 00:17:44.296
we're sort of past this thing

00:17:44.296 --> 00:17:45.998
and just let him
go at this point?

00:17:46.865 --> 00:17:48.333
- Well no,

00:17:48.333 --> 00:17:50.769 align:start size:97% position:3%
because the prosecutors
will be pounding the table.

00:17:50.769 --> 00:17:53.872
The appellate court
would have to find

00:17:53.872 --> 00:17:55.340
that the trial was unfair,

00:17:55.340 --> 00:17:59.445 align:start size:97% position:3%
that the trial judge made
mistakes of such a magnitude,

00:17:59.445 --> 00:18:00.979
that he was not proven guilty

00:18:00.979 --> 00:18:03.048
or that the sentence was unfair.

00:18:03.048 --> 00:18:04.516
The sentence was not unfair,

00:18:04.516 --> 00:18:07.686 align:start size:97% position:3%
it was well within the range
that the statute allows.

00:18:07.686 --> 00:18:10.756
So his only hope is to find

00:18:10.756 --> 00:18:13.592 align:start size:84% position:16%
that because the
first prosecutor said

00:18:13.592 --> 00:18:15.527
I'm not gonna prosecute you,

00:18:15.527 --> 00:18:18.097
they shouldn't have assigned
it to a second prosecutor.

00:18:18.097 --> 00:18:21.066
That's his only hope on appeal

00:18:21.066 --> 00:18:22.668
that once a prosecutor said,

00:18:22.668 --> 00:18:24.670
we're not prosecuting you,

00:18:24.670 --> 00:18:27.506
that that was it.

00:18:27.506 --> 00:18:29.241
Most states once
a prosecutor says

00:18:29.241 --> 00:18:30.542
we're not gonna prosecute you,

00:18:30.542 --> 00:18:32.544
they don't send it to
another prosecutor.

00:18:32.544 --> 00:18:35.080
They did it because Jussie

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:40.085
became very, very unpopular
in the Chicago community.

00:18:40.953 --> 00:18:41.887
Now you shouldn't be prosecuted

00:18:41.887 --> 00:18:43.388
just because you're unpopular.

00:18:43.388 --> 00:18:44.890
You should be prosecuted
if there's enough evidence

00:18:44.890 --> 00:18:48.193
to convict and if your
behavior has caused harm.

00:18:49.495 --> 00:18:51.597
- Where does that, that.

00:18:51.597 --> 00:18:53.365
If your behavior
has caused harm,

00:18:53.365 --> 00:18:56.635
where does that go when it
comes to the prosecuting?

00:18:56.635 --> 00:19:01.006 align:start size:97% position:3%
Meaning that in terms of the
actual crime he committed

00:19:01.006 --> 00:19:02.474
to set up this hoax,

00:19:02.474 --> 00:19:04.209
there's not a lot
of damage done,

00:19:04.209 --> 00:19:06.678
but I think you could argue
that there was a tremendous

00:19:06.678 --> 00:19:09.448
amount of damage
done to society,

00:19:09.448 --> 00:19:10.949
to the city of Chicago,

00:19:10.949 --> 00:19:13.285
but the whole country in
terms of this culture war

00:19:13.285 --> 00:19:14.720
that we're fighting,

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:16.688 align:start size:97% position:3%
how often do you think
judges are affected by that?

00:19:16.688 --> 00:19:19.691
That there's a much
bigger fight at play

00:19:19.691 --> 00:19:21.827
than the specifics of, oh, okay.

00:19:21.827 --> 00:19:23.362
He paid these two guys.

00:19:23.362 --> 00:19:25.764
He made up this thing, that
we could kind of look past,

00:19:25.764 --> 00:19:27.633
but it's really that
you could argue that

00:19:27.633 --> 00:19:29.368
then there were more
riots because of this,

00:19:29.368 --> 00:19:32.004 align:start size:88% position:13%
or just the general
flame of the culture war

00:19:32.004 --> 00:19:33.705
continues because of
something like this.

00:19:33.705 --> 00:19:36.441
- So that cannot be
taken into account

00:19:36.441 --> 00:19:37.509
at the time of sentencing.

00:19:37.509 --> 00:19:39.111
But here's something that can be

00:19:39.111 --> 00:19:40.279
that I think will interest you

00:19:40.279 --> 00:19:42.548 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the folks
listening to us now.

00:19:42.548 --> 00:19:44.049 align:start size:84% position:16%
When I told you
there's a checklist,

00:19:44.049 --> 00:19:47.519
25 aggravating and 25
mitigating factors.

00:19:47.519 --> 00:19:51.256 align:start size:97% position:3%
One of the aggravating factors
is the defendant committed

00:19:51.256 --> 00:19:56.261
this crime as a cost
of doing business.

00:19:57.663 --> 00:19:59.865 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now that was put in there
because of mafia guys

00:19:59.865 --> 00:20:03.068
who committed crimes as
a cost of doing business.

00:20:03.068 --> 00:20:05.404 align:start size:88% position:13%
And the judge in my
opinion, quite properly,

00:20:05.404 --> 00:20:07.906
did check that box.

00:20:07.906 --> 00:20:10.409
Jussie concocted
this whole thing

00:20:10.409 --> 00:20:12.511
as a cost of doing business.

00:20:12.511 --> 00:20:15.747 align:start size:88% position:13%
Now, he and I ultimately
had the same bosses

00:20:15.747 --> 00:20:18.150
because we both worked for Fox.

00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:20.786
I mean he's crazy to think
that he's gonna get a leg up

00:20:20.786 --> 00:20:24.122 align:start size:84% position:16%
at Fox by concocting
this nonsense,

00:20:24.122 --> 00:20:25.557
maybe at another network,

00:20:25.557 --> 00:20:26.925
but not at Fox.

00:20:26.925 --> 00:20:28.927
They'd be sorry
that it happened,

00:20:28.927 --> 00:20:33.131
but that's not going to,
not gonna help the careers.

00:20:33.131 --> 00:20:34.700
But back to your question,

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:37.302
the only harm was
to the government.

00:20:37.302 --> 00:20:41.940
The only harm traceable to
his behavior was $150,000

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:44.810 align:start size:84% position:16%
in police overtime
investigating an event

00:20:44.810 --> 00:20:46.612
that never occurred.

00:20:46.612 --> 00:20:49.781 line:0%
Now he was with six lies
because they interrogated

00:20:49.781 --> 00:20:53.585 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
him six times and
each time he gave them

00:20:53.585 --> 00:20:55.787 line:0%
the same nonsensical story.

00:20:55.787 --> 00:20:57.222
He was convicted of five.

00:20:57.222 --> 00:21:00.626 align:start size:94% position:6%
We won't know why without
asking the jurors,

00:21:00.626 --> 00:21:04.196
the sixth one did not
result in a conviction.

00:21:04.196 --> 00:21:07.499
Each of those could have
been three years in jail

00:21:07.499 --> 00:21:09.935
and he ended up with 150 days.

00:21:11.370 --> 00:21:13.038
- All right so let's shift to
a couple of the other things

00:21:13.038 --> 00:21:14.273
going on right now.

00:21:14.273 --> 00:21:17.309
So obviously Joe
Biden has selected

00:21:17.309 --> 00:21:20.445 align:start size:84% position:16%
Ketanji Brown Jackson
to be his nominee

00:21:20.445 --> 00:21:22.214
for the Supreme Court.

00:21:22.214 --> 00:21:26.118
I have no doubt that this
once the hearings begin

00:21:26.118 --> 00:21:27.552
will be as chaotic as ever,

00:21:27.552 --> 00:21:30.022 align:start size:94% position:6%
maybe not quite as chaotic
as Brett Kavanaugh,

00:21:30.022 --> 00:21:32.224
'cause she's kind of on
the side of the media,

00:21:32.224 --> 00:21:34.660
but the usual stuff
is gonna happen.

00:21:34.660 --> 00:21:36.161
- Yep.

00:21:36.161 --> 00:21:39.865
- I guess A, what would your
general qualifications be?

00:21:39.865 --> 00:21:41.733 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think I have a
sense of that already.

00:21:41.733 --> 00:21:44.436
And B, how do we pilfer
some truth out of this,

00:21:44.436 --> 00:21:46.471
knowing that the media
is not gonna cover it

00:21:46.471 --> 00:21:51.410
the way it would if say she
was nominated by Donald Trump

00:21:51.410 --> 00:21:53.278
or a Republican president.

00:21:54.746 --> 00:21:57.115
- I start with the premise
that under the constitution,

00:21:57.115 --> 00:21:59.217 align:start size:94% position:6%
the president gets to
nominate whoever he wants

00:21:59.217 --> 00:22:00.786
and believe it or not, Dave,

00:22:00.786 --> 00:22:03.922 align:start size:88% position:13%
neither the constitution
nor a federal law

00:22:03.922 --> 00:22:08.126 align:start size:97% position:3%
require that a Supreme Court
Justice even be a lawyer,

00:22:08.126 --> 00:22:12.631
much less held prior
judicial experience.

00:22:12.631 --> 00:22:15.767
The last justice who didn't
even graduate from law school

00:22:15.767 --> 00:22:17.269
was Justice Robert Jackson,

00:22:17.269 --> 00:22:19.438 align:start size:97% position:3%
who had been the attorney
general of the United States

00:22:19.438 --> 00:22:20.806
under FDR.

00:22:20.806 --> 00:22:24.276
And he is one of the
most quotable justices

00:22:24.276 --> 00:22:25.444
in the modern era,

00:22:25.444 --> 00:22:27.846
never graduated from law school.

00:22:27.846 --> 00:22:31.717
Today of course the Senate
would not confirm anyone

00:22:31.717 --> 00:22:34.219
who didn't graduate
from law school.

00:22:35.554 --> 00:22:38.390 align:start size:94% position:6%
The Senate and the
presidents seem to prefer

00:22:38.390 --> 00:22:41.093 align:start size:84% position:16%
people who have
judicial experience.

00:22:41.093 --> 00:22:44.463
That's not required at all.

00:22:44.463 --> 00:22:48.767
Chief Justice Earl Warren
had no judicial experience

00:22:48.767 --> 00:22:52.971 align:start size:97% position:3%
and probably affected
jurisprudence in this country

00:22:52.971 --> 00:22:53.939
more than anybody.

00:22:53.939 --> 00:22:55.474
Chief Justice John Marshall,

00:22:55.474 --> 00:22:58.710
the great chief justice
from the Thomas Jefferson

00:22:58.710 --> 00:23:00.078
to the Andrew Jackson years.

00:23:00.078 --> 00:23:03.115
He had no judicial experience.

00:23:03.115 --> 00:23:05.317 align:start size:84% position:16%
And because Joe
Biden nominated her,

00:23:05.317 --> 00:23:08.253
she's a liberal Democrat
that's to be expected.

00:23:08.253 --> 00:23:10.188
Donald Trump, I'm very familiar

00:23:10.188 --> 00:23:12.624
with his nomination processes.

00:23:12.624 --> 00:23:15.293
I was intimately involved
the first time around.

00:23:16.161 --> 00:23:18.196
Donald Trump, in my opinion,

00:23:18.196 --> 00:23:20.899 align:start size:97% position:3%
nominated two conservatives
and one libertarian.

00:23:20.899 --> 00:23:23.402
Libertarian is Neil Gorsuch.

00:23:23.402 --> 00:23:25.070 align:start size:84% position:16%
The conservatives
are Amy Coney Barrett

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:28.073
who is a graduate of the
same law school as I am,

00:23:28.073 --> 00:23:30.375
University of Notre Dame.

00:23:30.375 --> 00:23:32.477
And Brett Kavanaugh.

00:23:32.477 --> 00:23:36.381 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's the president's
prerogative.

00:23:36.381 --> 00:23:39.217 align:start position:0%
I don't agree with Senator
Lindsey Graham and many things,

00:23:39.217 --> 00:23:40.752
but I agree with him on this.

00:23:40.752 --> 00:23:42.320 align:start size:84% position:16%
The president
nominated the person

00:23:42.320 --> 00:23:44.156 align:start size:84% position:16%
and he or she is
basically qualified.

00:23:44.156 --> 00:23:45.857
I'm going to vote for them.

00:23:45.857 --> 00:23:48.026
I think that's what
the outcome will be

00:23:48.026 --> 00:23:50.762 align:start size:81% position:19%
with Judge Ketanji
Brown Jackson.

00:23:50.762 --> 00:23:54.733 align:start size:88% position:13%
However, there are
conservative Republicans

00:23:54.733 --> 00:23:57.836 align:start size:94% position:6%
on the judiciary committee
and I know them both.

00:23:57.836 --> 00:24:00.105
Well Ted Cruz have been a
friend of mine for years.

00:24:00.105 --> 00:24:04.009 align:start size:97% position:3%
Josh Hawley, we have met
through the monitor like you

00:24:04.009 --> 00:24:05.777
and I are meeting now.

00:24:05.777 --> 00:24:07.979
They're going to
go for her jugular.

00:24:07.979 --> 00:24:11.750 align:start size:88% position:13%
They're going to find
decisions that she made,

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:14.786 align:start position:0%
which she believes she was
compelled to make under the law,

00:24:14.786 --> 00:24:18.490 align:start size:94% position:6%
which appear inexplicable
to the public.

00:24:18.490 --> 00:24:21.726
Not to hurt her, not
to deny her the job,

00:24:21.726 --> 00:24:24.095
because she'll certainly get it.

00:24:24.095 --> 00:24:26.431
But to embarrass the president.

00:24:26.431 --> 00:24:29.201
This started with Robert Bork.

00:24:29.201 --> 00:24:30.869 align:start size:81% position:19%
- It was Joe Biden
who started it.

00:24:30.869 --> 00:24:33.605
- High gear with
Clarence Thomas.

00:24:35.140 --> 00:24:37.309
And then of course,
Brett Kavanaugh,

00:24:37.309 --> 00:24:39.010
it doesn't happen
to all of them.

00:24:39.010 --> 00:24:40.946
It didn't happen to
Amy Coney Barrett.

00:24:40.946 --> 00:24:43.715
It did not happen to
Ruth Bader Ginsburg.

00:24:43.715 --> 00:24:46.418
It did not happen
to Neil Gorsuch,

00:24:46.418 --> 00:24:47.886
but every once in a while,

00:24:47.886 --> 00:24:51.623 align:start size:88% position:13%
there is a nominee that
the minority party,

00:24:51.623 --> 00:24:53.992
that's the party that's
not in the White House,

00:24:53.992 --> 00:24:57.729
thinks they can tarnish the
person who did the nomination

00:24:57.729 --> 00:25:00.365
by tarnishing the candidate.

00:25:00.365 --> 00:25:04.002 align:start size:84% position:16%
History has shown that
does not carry over

00:25:04.002 --> 00:25:08.306
into the nominees'
judicial opinions,

00:25:08.306 --> 00:25:11.076
whatever you think
of Clarence Thomas,

00:25:11.076 --> 00:25:13.245 align:start size:84% position:16%
he has either
forgiven or forgotten.

00:25:13.245 --> 00:25:16.681
And the same thing
with Brett Kavanaugh.

00:25:16.681 --> 00:25:20.018
Robert Bork of course
the trashing worked.

00:25:20.018 --> 00:25:24.756
He was rejected by a vote of
52 against and 48 in favor.

00:25:24.756 --> 00:25:27.959 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah which Joe Biden
basically led the charge on.

00:25:27.959 --> 00:25:29.194
- Yes he did.

00:25:29.194 --> 00:25:30.395
- Do you think that
there's some value

00:25:30.395 --> 00:25:32.764
in say Ted Cruz, Josh Hawley,

00:25:32.764 --> 00:25:35.567
some of these other guys
going for the jugular,

00:25:35.567 --> 00:25:37.302
even if she's gonna be
confirmed either way

00:25:37.302 --> 00:25:40.639
just to at least expose
some of the thinking

00:25:40.639 --> 00:25:42.073
behind the decisions

00:25:42.073 --> 00:25:44.809 align:start size:88% position:13%
so that people can frame
the issues properly,

00:25:44.809 --> 00:25:46.611
even if they can't stop it.

00:25:46.611 --> 00:25:47.846
Even though, and of course,

00:25:47.846 --> 00:25:49.114
it all becomes a
circus either way.

00:25:49.114 --> 00:25:52.050
- I think the value is
to the political base.

00:25:52.050 --> 00:25:53.218
- Yeah.

00:25:53.218 --> 00:25:55.320 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Most respectfully
to my friends,

00:25:55.320 --> 00:25:58.523
for Ted Cruz and Josh Hawley.

00:25:58.523 --> 00:26:02.627
I don't think there's
any value to the public

00:26:02.627 --> 00:26:05.330
in having a circuit
court judge explain

00:26:05.330 --> 00:26:08.600
the intricacies of
a judicial opinion,

00:26:08.600 --> 00:26:13.104 align:start size:97% position:3%
which really only law school
graduates can comprehend.

00:26:13.104 --> 00:26:15.140
A lot of times I made
decisions on the bench

00:26:15.140 --> 00:26:17.509
that the law forced me to make,

00:26:17.509 --> 00:26:20.679
that I sent people
to jail for crimes

00:26:20.679 --> 00:26:22.380 align:start size:81% position:19%
that in my opinion
were victimless

00:26:22.380 --> 00:26:23.615
and shouldn't have been crimes

00:26:23.615 --> 00:26:25.116
and they should not
have gone to jail.

00:26:25.116 --> 00:26:28.253
Like the three strikes on
possession of marijuana,

00:26:28.253 --> 00:26:29.387
he went away for 20 years.

00:26:29.387 --> 00:26:30.789
That's so absurd,

00:26:30.789 --> 00:26:33.758
especially today where
it's legal in 37 states.

00:26:33.758 --> 00:26:36.394 align:start size:88% position:13%
But there are times when
judges must do things

00:26:36.394 --> 00:26:38.964
that they don't want to do
because they've taken an oath

00:26:38.964 --> 00:26:41.433
to uphold the law and
the law requires it.

00:26:41.433 --> 00:26:45.036
Explaining that so that the
public can understand it

00:26:45.036 --> 00:26:47.639
is a very difficult task.

00:26:47.639 --> 00:26:49.841 align:start size:88% position:13%
- What about if the
judge was truly radical

00:26:49.841 --> 00:26:52.143
and I honestly don't
know enough about her,

00:26:52.143 --> 00:26:53.845 align:start size:97% position:3%
her full resume and obviously
we're gonna hear it,

00:26:53.845 --> 00:26:57.349 align:start size:97% position:3%
but what if they were someone
that really did not hold

00:26:57.349 --> 00:26:59.517 align:start size:97% position:3%
any of the principles that
you mentioned at the beginning

00:26:59.517 --> 00:27:01.686 align:start size:97% position:3%
about our founding documents
and individual rights

00:27:01.686 --> 00:27:04.389
and they really were
activist in terms of

00:27:04.389 --> 00:27:06.391 align:start size:84% position:16%
they wanted to
fundamentally change

00:27:06.391 --> 00:27:08.994
the way that the country is run.

00:27:08.994 --> 00:27:11.930 align:start size:97% position:3%
You'd want some exploratory
version of it, right?

00:27:11.930 --> 00:27:13.365
- Well the answer is yes.

00:27:13.365 --> 00:27:15.700
However, with Judge
Jackson, I mean,

00:27:15.700 --> 00:27:18.269 align:start size:97% position:3%
she was confirmed to the
United States District Court

00:27:18.269 --> 00:27:21.172
eight years ago and
then was confirmed

00:27:21.172 --> 00:27:25.977
by this very same senate to
the Circuit Court of Appeals

00:27:25.977 --> 00:27:27.545
one year ago.

00:27:27.545 --> 00:27:29.080 align:start size:84% position:16%
So they've already
gone through all this.

00:27:29.080 --> 00:27:33.885 align:start size:88% position:13%
However, if some
nominee did not believe

00:27:33.885 --> 00:27:37.422
that the constitution was
the supreme law of the land

00:27:37.422 --> 00:27:40.592 align:start size:97% position:3%
or believed that the
Declaration of Independence

00:27:40.592 --> 00:27:43.261
was just Jefferson's musings

00:27:43.261 --> 00:27:46.831
or that the first amendment
really doesn't guarantee

00:27:46.831 --> 00:27:47.999
the freedom of speech,

00:27:47.999 --> 00:27:49.668 align:start size:88% position:13%
that person would
probably be disqualified

00:27:49.668 --> 00:27:52.804 align:start size:81% position:19%
and those views
should be exposed.

00:27:53.972 --> 00:27:56.207 align:start size:84% position:16%
- All right so let's
move on to Russia

00:27:56.207 --> 00:27:58.476 align:start size:81% position:19%
because you've
taken the position

00:27:58.476 --> 00:28:01.646 align:start size:88% position:13%
that I fully respect
because it's hard to get

00:28:01.646 --> 00:28:04.082
any sort of sense out
of what's going on

00:28:04.082 --> 00:28:05.583
with Russia and Ukraine,

00:28:05.583 --> 00:28:10.188
that sanctions actually
violate the constitution.

00:28:10.188 --> 00:28:12.257
So can you explain that a bit?

00:28:12.257 --> 00:28:13.758
- Sure.

00:28:13.758 --> 00:28:16.961
And this is not anti-Joe Biden.

00:28:16.961 --> 00:28:19.531
I honestly believe he's
doing everything he can

00:28:20.465 --> 00:28:23.768
short of war to resist Putin.

00:28:25.203 --> 00:28:27.305 align:start size:94% position:6%
I also believe the Putin's
ultimately going to win

00:28:27.305 --> 00:28:29.507 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that by our
arming the Ukrainians

00:28:29.507 --> 00:28:31.543
we're probably making it worse,

00:28:31.543 --> 00:28:34.512
bloodier and more deadly.

00:28:34.512 --> 00:28:38.883 align:start size:97% position:3%
However, the sanctions
fall into three categories,

00:28:38.883 --> 00:28:43.755
seizing assets, freezing assets,

00:28:44.823 --> 00:28:47.525
and interfering with contracts.

00:28:47.525 --> 00:28:48.827
So under the fourth amendment,

00:28:48.827 --> 00:28:52.530 align:start size:88% position:13%
if the government
freezes a bank account,

00:28:52.530 --> 00:28:53.965
they freeze your bank
account for example,

00:28:53.965 --> 00:28:57.302 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you can't take any
money out, that's a seizure.

00:28:57.302 --> 00:28:58.536
And under the fourth amendment,

00:28:58.536 --> 00:29:00.271
the government can
only seize something

00:29:00.271 --> 00:29:03.675
with a search warrant
after they demonstrate

00:29:03.675 --> 00:29:05.310
to a judge like I was,

00:29:05.310 --> 00:29:07.245
I signed hundreds of these,

00:29:07.245 --> 00:29:10.248
probable cause of crime.

00:29:10.248 --> 00:29:13.051 align:start size:97% position:3%
So if they seize your
yacht in the Bay of Naples,

00:29:13.051 --> 00:29:15.954
if the Carabinieri which
is the Italian version

00:29:15.954 --> 00:29:19.190 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the FBI seizes your
yacht in the Bay of Naples

00:29:19.190 --> 00:29:20.658
and they won't let you on it.

00:29:20.658 --> 00:29:23.962 align:start size:97% position:3%
And they've sent it to Port
Newark so that the feds

00:29:23.962 --> 00:29:27.165
can actually own it and sell it.

00:29:27.165 --> 00:29:29.567
They have seized your
property illegally.

00:29:30.769 --> 00:29:32.137
If they've frozen
your bank account

00:29:32.137 --> 00:29:33.738
without a search warrant,

00:29:33.738 --> 00:29:37.041
they have frozen your
bank account illegally.

00:29:37.041 --> 00:29:40.178
If they have interfered with
lawful commercial transactions,

00:29:40.178 --> 00:29:43.748 align:start size:97% position:3%
if, for example, you're an
importer of Stolichnaya vodka,

00:29:43.748 --> 00:29:47.418
and you've paid for a cargo
ship full of Stolichnaya,

00:29:47.418 --> 00:29:50.889 align:start size:97% position:3%
and it arrives at Port
Newark or Chicago or Detroit,

00:29:50.889 --> 00:29:53.558
and the feds send it away,

00:29:53.558 --> 00:29:55.760 align:start size:81% position:19%
well then the feds
have interfered

00:29:55.760 --> 00:29:58.263 align:start size:88% position:13%
with your lawful
commercial transactions.

00:29:58.263 --> 00:30:00.999
The fifth amendment says if
the government wants life,

00:30:00.999 --> 00:30:03.234
liberty, contractual rights

00:30:03.234 --> 00:30:06.471
or property income
from the contract,

00:30:06.471 --> 00:30:09.741 align:start size:97% position:3%
they have to have a jury trial
at which fault is proven.

00:30:09.741 --> 00:30:12.410
Now under what basis
is President Biden

00:30:12.410 --> 00:30:13.678
engaging these sanctions?

00:30:13.678 --> 00:30:15.947
There's two statutes, Dave,

00:30:15.947 --> 00:30:17.715
that the feds enacted.

00:30:17.715 --> 00:30:21.953 align:start position:0%
One is the International
Economic Emergency Act of 1977,

00:30:22.887 --> 00:30:24.556
Carter era.

00:30:24.556 --> 00:30:29.561
One is the Magnitsky
Act of 2016, Obama era.

00:30:29.561 --> 00:30:32.430 align:start size:84% position:16%
Both of these statutes
allow the president

00:30:32.430 --> 00:30:36.334 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
without any evidence or
judicial process whatsoever

00:30:36.334 --> 00:30:39.771 line:0%
to declare a human
being or an entity,

00:30:39.771 --> 00:30:43.174
foreign human being,
or foreign entity,

00:30:43.174 --> 00:30:47.111 align:start size:94% position:6%
a violator of human rights
and seize their assets.

00:30:47.111 --> 00:30:48.780
And if they want
the assets back,

00:30:48.780 --> 00:30:51.749 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you're this billionaire
whose yacht was stolen

00:30:51.749 --> 00:30:53.551
by the Carabinieri
and you want it back,

00:30:53.551 --> 00:30:55.587
you have to come to America,

00:30:55.587 --> 00:30:58.056
submit to the federal
court jurisdiction,

00:30:58.056 --> 00:30:59.324
which they're never gonna do,

00:30:59.324 --> 00:31:00.458
because they're afraid
they'll get arrested

00:31:00.458 --> 00:31:01.893
for some trumped up charge.

00:31:01.893 --> 00:31:04.729
And then you have to
prove that you are not

00:31:05.830 --> 00:31:07.131
a human rights violator.

00:31:07.131 --> 00:31:09.234
How do you prove a negative?

00:31:09.234 --> 00:31:11.069
And that's "Alice
in Wonderland."

00:31:11.069 --> 00:31:12.237
You're in a federal court.

00:31:12.237 --> 00:31:13.872 align:start size:84% position:16%
The government
wants your property,

00:31:13.872 --> 00:31:16.274 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you have to prove
that you're not what
they say you are?

00:31:16.274 --> 00:31:17.508
Forget about it.

00:31:17.508 --> 00:31:19.677
The government has
the burden of proof.

00:31:19.677 --> 00:31:21.646
The person whose life,
liberty, or property

00:31:21.646 --> 00:31:25.516 align:start size:97% position:3%
the government wants
always is presumed innocent

00:31:25.516 --> 00:31:28.786
and to presume to be the
valid owner of the life,

00:31:28.786 --> 00:31:30.822
liberty, or property.

00:31:30.822 --> 00:31:32.690
So these two statutes,

00:31:32.690 --> 00:31:36.127 align:start size:97% position:3%
which have not been
challenged constitutionally,

00:31:36.127 --> 00:31:38.129
because these foreigners
are not gonna come here

00:31:38.129 --> 00:31:39.831
to challenge them,

00:31:39.831 --> 00:31:41.900
give the president
dictatorial powers,

00:31:41.900 --> 00:31:43.968
which he has exercised.

00:31:43.968 --> 00:31:45.870 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Why do you think
some of our companies,

00:31:45.870 --> 00:31:48.573 align:start size:84% position:16%
maybe that do
business with Russia

00:31:48.573 --> 00:31:50.475 align:start size:84% position:16%
